{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate an implicit constraint graph—relationships between components that are never documented as APIs or types but are nevertheless enforced by the system's runtime behavior. Each locally correct change optimizes against explicit specifications while silently tightening this hidden graph, so the system's true coupling complexity grows superlinearly even as its measured complexity (e.g., cyclomatic, LOC) grows linearly. The difficulty of change scales with the size and density of this undocumented graph, not the code's surface structure, which is why degradation is invisible to standard metrics until modifications begin violating constraints the team never knew existed."}
